WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:06.580
Welcome to the MIT Center for
Art, Science &amp; Technology's

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.870
2021 Symposium
"Unfolding Intelligence:

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:13.590
the Art and Science of
Contemporary Computation".

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.910
CAST was established
in 2012 with the goal

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.470
of building and
building on connections

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.880
between the worlds of art,
science, and technology.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.200
This is the third in
a series of symposia

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.790
that CAST has
convened since then.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.900
And as with its predecessors,
we bring together

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:33.360
artists, scientists, engineers,
and humanists from within MIT

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.690
and from the world
at large to discuss

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.650
areas of
rapidly-evolving research

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.610
and urgent social relevance.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.230
And to find, in that dialogue,
stimulation, confirmation,

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:48.680
provocation, intersection,
and, we hope, common purpose.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.720
At MIT, CAST partners with
departments, labs, and centers

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.800
to integrate the arts
across the curriculum,

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.600
to enrich and encourage artistic
collaboration, and to provide

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.630
support to faculty and
members of the MIT community

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:04.030
as they pursue their
own artistic practice

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.910
and/or research.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.840
In addition to
symposia like this,

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.470
CAST facilitates the sharing
of this creative work

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.030
beyond the Institute
by producing concerts,

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:16.640
exhibitions, and
publications, and making

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.500
them available to the public.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.690
So thank you for
being with us today.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.940
We hope you will join
us throughout the week

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.300
at virtual events addressing
the aesthetic, technical,

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.590
and critical issues pertaining
to artificial intelligence

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.470
and computational media.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:35.720
We also look forward to
seeing you on Friday,

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.350
April 9th as the
symposium culminates

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.910
with a live interactive
event to which

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:42.640
all attendees are invited.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.320
And which you can join
presenters and artists

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.810
in breakout rooms to explore
hidden threads between all that

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.290
has been discussed this week.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.920
Hi, I'm very glad
to be with you today

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.360
to talk about the "Generative
Unfoldings" exhibit.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.180
I'm Nick Montfort, and
I want to introduce

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:16.430
my colleague, Fox
Harrell from MIT here;

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:20.620
Lauren Lee McCarthy, from UCLA;
and Sarah Rosalena Brady,

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.510
from UC-Santa Barbara.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:27.080
Also, I want to extend
thanks to Parag Mital, who

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.140
couldn't be with us for
this conversation today.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.780
But all of us participated
in the conversation

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:36.660
about how to select works for
commissions for this project.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.570
And this project
manifests itself

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:43.960
as a generative art exhibit with
works running in the browser,

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.690
but also is a free
software exhibit

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:53.670
that allows people to
study, fork, distribute,

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:59.310
rework any of the projects
that have been done in it.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.690
And this is all
thanks to the Center

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:05.700
for Art, Science, and
Technology here at MIT.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.530
And in relation to
the generative--

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:10.600
this "Generative
Unfoldings" exhibit

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.700
is in relation to the "Unfolding
Intelligence" symposium.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.960
We weren't able to
do what we've done

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.650
in the past with symposia--
bring people physically

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:25.750
to campus, have panels and
good in-person engagement.

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:29.360
So we decided to reach out
to the community of artists

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.700
and bring some of
their work online

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.710
in ways that it could
live in the browser

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:35.920
and run in the browser.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:41.720
But it could also be part
of future collaborations

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.000
and work with projects
of generative sorts.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:54.170
So maybe the best question
to start off with in relation

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:56.800
to the artworks themselves, the
commissions that we awarded,

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.300
and the pieces that
we ended up with here,

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.200
is about what generative
art exactly is,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.010
because I think
there's a stereotype

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.000
or a concept of generative art.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.190
It's a new concept that we have.

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:12.880
It's not like painting or
sculpture that's been around

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:15.580
with us for centuries.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:19.410
But there's already some
type of typical idea

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.560
of what generative art is.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.770
And one of the things that
excites me about the show

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:26.720
is that it's certainly not
unrelated to that idea.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.440
But I think there's a lot
of interesting variation

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.880
and diversity in the
works that we see.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.470
Maybe I'll ask
Lauren if you'd like

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:41.450
to talk about what generative
art maybe has been more

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:44.380
typically or has been
thought of and if you

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.300
see trends in the
show that challenge

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:51.060
or expand that in
some interesting ways.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:51.560
Sure.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:58.110
I mean, I think generative
art in the most basic sense

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:01.830
could be thought of as
art that's made with code

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:07.740
and that is constantly
generating or constantly making

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.810
a new form with itself.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.760
So it's different than
a video or an image,

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:16.215
where it's a closed work
that you could loop or view

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:18.090
multiple times, but it
always stays the same.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.910
so with a generative
work, there's

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:23.280
a quality of liveness,
where it's always changing.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.910
And often, you might
see an iteration

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:30.440
that's never been seen before
in the playthrough of the work,

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:32.550
because it's live
running software.

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.980
However, I think
that in the past

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:39.130
that our conception of what
might fit into generative art

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:41.850
has been more limited in terms
of the sorts of aesthetics

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.267
that we're seeing,
or the

00:05:43.267 --> 00:05:49.060
types of moves or
techniques used with code.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.190
And I think what's
interesting about this--

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:56.180
and often, it's focused a lot
on the very visual, formal realm

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:56.700
of things.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.117
And I think what's
really exciting

00:05:58.117 --> 00:05:59.840
about this exhibition
is that it really

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:02.990
spans a wide range of
media and techniques.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:07.520
And it's hard to find works
that actually even look

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:08.650
very similar to each other.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:12.690
They all go in really
different directions.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.620
Yeah, that's definitely one
of the things that I noticed.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:19.140
And in fact, from
my own standpoint,

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.150
as someone who has
a practice as a poet

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:24.770
and is involved with text
and language particularly,

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:25.790
it's interesting.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:29.020
Maybe I just attracted
those types of submissions.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.247
Or maybe that was
something that came out

00:06:31.247 --> 00:06:33.330
of my being part of the
conversation and so forth.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:37.710
But there's nine works
by my count, where text

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.850
is a really important aspect--

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:44.450
about nine of the 14.

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:47.910
And that is compatible
with an idea

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:50.880
of generative art,
obviously, but not something

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:56.870
that we imagine in maybe the
stereotypical geometric play

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:00.280
of shapes running by themselves.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:03.190
And I should say also,
speaking of things

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:10.210
that run by themselves, that's
an idea that is not absolutely

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:11.690
defining in generative art.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.800
But I recently was teaching
from Margaret Boden

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.290
and Ernest Edmonds'
book, Fingers to Digits,

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:21.220
and they defined this
concept of CG art,

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:24.200
giving everything a
name in the typology.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:31.380
And so this is what we really
mean by "generative art" here.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.690
And the standard
central example of that

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:36.890
is stuff that runs by itself.

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:39.480
So they don't preclude
things that are interactive.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.620
But you really
should-- the piece

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:44.450
should really run by itself
if it really definitely wants

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:47.070
to be generative art.

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:51.880
And so we also have lots of
stuff that's interactive.

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:55.110
And that's interesting
to me that there

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:58.360
are some things where
interactivity is optional.

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:02.170
There are some things,
though, that do run, but allow

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:05.830
for intervention.

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:07.850
Did you want to
make any comments,

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:14.380
say, Fox, about that aspect or
another aspect of the artworks

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:15.960
themselves in the exhibit?

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:16.540
Yeah 

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:17.770
I think that

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:19.350
both Lauren and you have made

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:20.310
made such great points here.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.169
One of the things that

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.045
I can add, in addition to

00:08:24.045 --> 00:08:26.330
the computer-generated
aspect of this kind of work,

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:29.560
is looking at it in
art historical context.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:31.770
So for me, it relates
not only to works

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.460
that run autonomously
or computationally,

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:38.039
but also trends in other forms
of art, such as conceptual art,

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:40.900
where forms of conceptual
art, such as instruction art,

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:44.010
works within literary practice
such as from the group 

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:48.320
Oulipo doing experimental
role-constrained work and more.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.580
And so when I think
back to conceptual and

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.385
instruction-based artwork,
I see a similar kind

00:08:53.385 --> 00:08:55.260
of difference, where
you have some works that

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.020
are very formally focused.

00:08:57.020 --> 00:08:58.830
Like Sol LeWitt's
work, for example,

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:00.880
tends to be highly formal.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:04.720
But you also have artists such
as Adrian Piper or Yoko Ono

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:08.040
that do work that, in one
case, is more politically

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:10.370
engaged and socially engaged.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:12.040
In another case,
I would say it's

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:14.330
more politically and
socially engaged,

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:18.660
too, in terms of 
say, Yoko Ono's instruction

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.750
art for paintings-- that,
"Cut a hole in a canvas.

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:24.490
Hold it up to a sky"--
this kind of work.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:26.500
And so I think the
idea of instructions

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:29.260
that will be used to
generate some work,

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:33.210
whether they're actually
implemented or just conceptual,

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:36.270
I think is quite related to
this kind of show and this idea

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:39.100
that we have artists that
work in a poetic vein,

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:42.850
in a sociopolitical
vein, and more, using

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:44.740
instruction-based or
algorithmic techniques

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:47.510
is something that's unique
about this particular show.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:49.720
I should say, at least,
it's underrepresented

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.810
when compared to a lot
of generative artwork

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:55.860
that tends to be more
formally focused.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.240
Yeah, I think there's a
range of work, some of which

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:03.480
is quite overtly engaged with
the power and susceptibility

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:06.860
of the gaze with
surveillance, even

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:09.630
with self-doubting
and questioning

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:11.050
and things like this.

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:18.440
And other pieces that complicate
the highly formalist sorts

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:22.630
of projects of generative art--

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:26.680
those are a few of the
trends that I noticed

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:28.980
in the artwork that
maybe make this different

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:34.200
than the overall stereotype.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:37.570
Sarah, do you want to add to
that from your perspective

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:41.400
in terms of how the
artworks look to you?

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.690
Yeah, no, and I
completely agree with Fox.

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:47.530
A lot of relationship
between Dada and Fluxus

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:50.230
and how a lot of the
works that we chose

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:54.600
include this formal instruction
as a way to produce.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.790
But because we're looking
at this in the future

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:01.530
and also focusing more on the
terms of AI, a lot of this

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.840
is pointed towards language
and decision-making

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:07.360
towards the unknown, which
I think is so interesting.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:10.110
This idea of generative art
or "generative unfoldings"

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:14.240
is how these works
also try to understand

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:18.170
what's hidden because what their
output is an emergent form.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:20.310
And I find that is
really important when

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:24.880
we have these really complex
conversations about how

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:29.180
these artworks are going
to be shaping our future

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:30.970
and how it could
be more inclusive.

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:35.730
And that, again, goes back
to this lineage of how

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:40.580
this idea of this output
of something emergent

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:44.410
is really unique and special
because it can really

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:48.020
get to the core root of what we
don't see and what is unknown,

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:52.120
and also have the unknown
and things that are alien

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:53.370
have power.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:55.760
And I'm curious what
you guys think, too.

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:03.350
Yeah, I mean-- go
ahead, Lauren, please.

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:05.150
Yeah, I would just
want to add I think

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:09.180
one thing I noticed with the
exhibition, the selected works,

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:12.630
was that we're talking about
these generative systems, which

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:13.630
is software.

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:16.420
But the human is really
present in a lot of the pieces.

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:18.850
And Nick, you were mentioning
the language coming through.

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:20.433
[00:10:31.42] And I
think that for me,

00:12:20.433 --> 00:12:21.980
that reads as a part of it.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:24.310
There's a real
questioning of, OK,

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:26.560
so we have these
systems around us.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:28.710
How do we interact
with them as humans?

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:32.240
How are they shaping
us as society?

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:35.320
In what ways is that
moving us towards a future

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:38.570
that we do or don't want to see,
to speak to Sarah's point, too.

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:42.360
Yeah.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:46.130
And also-- go ahead,
Fox, actually.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:46.900
Please.

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:48.560
Oh, I was just going
to add that I've

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:52.260
been long interested in how AI
and other kinds of expressive

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.570
systems encode
societies phantasms--

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:57.860
that is, in terms of the
social or the mental,

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:00.200
the sensory imagery,
that we engage

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:03.150
with that encodes worldview
of all different types,

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:04.530
including bias.

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:08.370
And one of the terms that's come
into common parlance these days

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:09.700
is "algorithmic bias."

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:14.260
That's a kind of concern that
we have with AI in general, not

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:15.300
just an anxiety.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:18.170
It's something that's a real
concern, especially as we've

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:21.480
moved more towards artificial
neural network-based systems

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:22.640
as being prevalent.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:25.320
So I think that a lot of
what Sarah is speaking about

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:30.150
is about that the kind of
implicit information that gets

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:33.160
built into these
kinds of systems.

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:36.100
But what I love about this
particular set of works

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:39.250
is that it's not only
about those kind of issues

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:42.570
around algorithmic
bias in the abstract.

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:46.390
But it's engaging with notions
such as feminist theory

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:49.850
or notions like the subaltern
and critique of hegemony

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:54.600
from cultural studies and
related kinds of research areas

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:58.060
and representations
and theories of gender

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:00.740
and so much more,
along with the formal.

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:03.810
And so I feel like that's
something which is especially

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:07.400
powerful, is to leverage this
generative algorithmic data

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:10.720
structural kind of approach--
engage deeply with AI,

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:13.190
but for these kind of issues--

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:18.490
feminism, the subaltern, gender,
and identity and society,

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:19.660
politics, and so much more.

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:27.870
Yeah, I think the range
of topics and issues

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:32.420
of cultural relevance that are
taken on is quite impressive,

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:35.030
and also the fact
that these aren't just

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:39.860
works that use a single
artistic method to do AI

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:43.200
to everything in the same way.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:51.800
They relate to visualizing,
to textualizing, to collaging,

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:56.160
to using a variety of
different processes related

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:59.650
to the way we think
about AI as it

00:14:59.650 --> 00:15:02.014
pertains to these other
cultural phenomena.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:11.040
But maybe we should talk
some about the human beings,

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:14.800
not just the computational
techniques, that

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:16.800
are involved with these
pieces, because they're

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:19.150
made by artists.

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:22.740
And I think we have an
interesting range of artists.

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.360
I don't want to be too
quantitative, like we often

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:29.780
are at MIT, and try to list
right now how many countries

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:31.820
and things like that.

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:35.640
But I do think it's
intriguing that we have people

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:41.520
at different stages of their
work, some who are doing really

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:45.110
the first project as an
art duo, some of whom

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:47.120
have quite mature
practices and have

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:49.340
made many different
contributions

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:50.890
in generative art.

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:59.690
We have people associated with
different schools and programs.

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:03.980
And we have people
who come to this

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:09.260
from many, many
different perspectives--

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:14.360
work individually in some cases,
collaborate in other cases.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:23.920
What do you all think about
the particular set of artists

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:29.820
and artist duos that
we have represented

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:33.170
and that have actually put
together this work that we're

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:33.670
looking at?

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:36.080
Does anyone want to
offer some comments

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:41.190
on the people behind the artwork
and what new sorts of trends

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:42.330
we see there?

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:50.070
One thing that I
was thinking about

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:54.620
was how these works fit into
the practice of the artists.

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:59.690
So I noticed for
some works, this work

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:02.480
that they have in
the exhibition is

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:06.560
part of a long series of works
in this direction that they've

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:07.990
been making.

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:12.730
And then for others, it's
actually a first or one

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:15.359
of the first forays into
generative art or making

00:17:15.359 --> 00:17:17.910
something that's primarily
web or software-based,

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:21.630
and maybe coming from a practice
that's more based in sculpture

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:25.210
or video or other media.

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:27.859
So I think that was
really exciting to see.

00:17:27.859 --> 00:17:29.380
And I wonder how
much of it also had

00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:31.970
to do with the pandemic
in general and the fact

00:17:31.970 --> 00:17:34.640
that a lot of artists are
questioning and thinking

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:39.070
about how their practices
live in this virtual space.

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:40.640
But I think because
of that, there

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:43.800
is a really nice energy
here, where there are--

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:47.410
the works are building on
communities or libraries

00:17:47.410 --> 00:17:47.970
that exist.

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:52.040
So it's very tied to
longstanding collaborative

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:54.490
traditions in making
generative art.

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:59.250
But there's also this
energy of new exploration

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:02.510
and new experiments
that feels very tangible

00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:03.460
in a lot of the works.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:08.660
Yeah.

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:10.470
Maybe I should also
ask that we say

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:13.570
a little bit about our own
creative practices, which

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:15.110
we all have.

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:19.440
We all come to this as
artists of some sort.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:23.920
And we also have our academic
affiliations under our belt

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:25.880
and so forth.

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:29.490
And I mentioned that I
do identify as a poet,

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:32.840
and my work is definitely
focused on language.

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:35.910
But for me, even
more computation

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:37.660
is important to what I do.

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:40.030
So creative computing
of various sorts,

00:18:40.030 --> 00:18:45.250
often dealing with language,
is central to my approach.

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:48.420
Sarah, will you tell us
about your art practice

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:51.150
and its relationship
to generative art?

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:53.810
Yeah, so I actually teach
computational craft,

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:57.430
which is a really unique
way of deconstructing

00:18:57.430 --> 00:18:59.430
and materializing technology.

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:03.070
But I really use it, especially
with AI and all these emergent

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:07.080
forms and making, as a way to
really break down and open up

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:08.860
new systems for
new possibilities

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:13.700
and have new conversations,
just because the historical root

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:16.020
of computation and code,
rooted in the military

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:16.690
and colonialism.

00:19:16.690 --> 00:19:18.090
It's really important
that we continue

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:19.673
to have these
conversations and evolve

00:19:19.673 --> 00:19:22.340
because these partial
perspectives create

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:23.640
objective vision.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:26.860
So we need to constantly be
including arts and humanities

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:29.050
and force the machine to
think outside the box.

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:31.240
And I feel like-- like
what Lauren just said.

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:32.520
This is the perfect moment.

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:34.103
I feel like people
are really starting

00:19:34.103 --> 00:19:36.450
to be aware of the systems
they participate in because

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:39.020
of the pandemic, because
technology has been

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:44.190
the crucial platform for
this whole perspective,

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:47.010
including us talking over Zoom.

00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:50.610
So I think that's
really important

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:55.080
because we are going to have to
break the systems in many ways.

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:57.080
And art has always done that.

00:19:57.080 --> 00:19:59.362
And that's why-- because
art and technology really

00:19:59.362 --> 00:20:00.570
do co-evolve with each other.

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:05.420
Yes.

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:08.520
And the moment for
this investigation--

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:11.560
it's not just an
opportunity to invite

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:15.300
some artists to put some things
online, although I think it is.

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:18.110
But it is a real
reflective moment

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.700
in dealing with
art and computing.

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:23.510
Fox, tell us some
about your engagement

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:29.320
as an artist as well
as your other roles

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:32.430
as a computer
scientist, for instance?

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:33.460
Sure, I'd be happy to.

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:37.520
So I think the way that I
approach this kind of work

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:41.800
is looking at the
computer as a medium,

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:44.430
and then more specifically,
certain types of techniques,

00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:46.970
like AI-based techniques--
looking at the way

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:49.200
that meaning is
represented and encoded

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:52.050
within computational systems,
and then leveraging it

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:56.540
for expression, especially
narrative, narrative imagining,

00:20:56.540 --> 00:21:01.110
this type of work that
has the kind of coherence

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:05.240
in terms of time, in terms
of causality, and so forth.

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:08.630
But really engaged
with social issues,

00:21:08.630 --> 00:21:14.170
with genre and other forms
of fiction at the same time.

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:17.040
And so to make that
more concrete is

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:22.130
to think about ways that, say,
an author in genre fiction

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:27.130
might leverage fantasy or
diverse forms of the imaginary

00:21:27.130 --> 00:21:30.380
for social critique
in very precise ways.

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:36.080
And I think about works not in
one particular kind of genre

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:37.800
or one particular
medium, but works

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:40.630
that have that kind of
quality across them.

00:21:40.630 --> 00:21:44.340
So that could be work like,
in literature, Ralph Ellison's

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:46.950
Invisible Man, which I
feel is one of the greatest

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:49.110
works of sociology
that just happens

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:52.090
to be in the form of a
highly metaphorical work

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:53.130
of literature.

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:55.900
But also, people like
Italo Calvino, I think,

00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:58.510
who uses highly
formal approaches

00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:03.650
to get really lush, poetic
kind of prose within the work.

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:07.620
And people like, say, Charles
Mingus or Nina Simone,

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:13.370
that leverage both
compositional strategies along

00:22:13.370 --> 00:22:17.440
with political engagement
to make really sometimes

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:20.160
incendiary and incisive
social statements.

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:24.280
So I really look for those
kinds [INAUDIBLE] takes the form

00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:29.380
[INAUDIBLE] of their games that
are pushing the expressive

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:32.400
potential of computational
games medium--

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:33.990
sometimes immersive
environments,

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:39.200
such as in virtual reality,
and sometimes novel forms

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:42.140
that incorporate
aspects of all of those,

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:45.370
but might not be
anticipated by just some

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:49.260
of those conventional labels.

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:52.110
Yeah, and when you came to
MIT, the lab you founded

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:54.980
is the Imagination,
Computation, and Expression Lab.

00:22:54.980 --> 00:23:02.390
And this idea that computing can
be expressive and imaginative--

00:23:02.390 --> 00:23:05.400
can offer the
phantasms that allow

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:08.900
us to work toward
a better society--

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:10.660
is a very compelling one.

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:13.880
A lot of my work is
not so expressive.

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:16.740
It's not much about the
things I wish to express,

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:21.700
but it's more about a conceptual
or cool sort of engagement

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:24.570
with language and
computation, which I hope then

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:29.810
resonates in a really compelling
and interesting way because

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:33.700
of all of the cultural histories
and linguistic histories

00:23:33.700 --> 00:23:37.350
and histories of computing that
it can't help but bounce up

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:38.350
against.

00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:41.720
But let me give Lauren a
chance to tell us some.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:45.780
I'm really eager to hear
her speak about her art work

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:48.180
as well.

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:49.130
Sure.

00:23:49.130 --> 00:23:51.870
Yeah, my practice is very
grounded in performance

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:52.560
and software.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:54.600
But it spans a range of media.

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:56.680
And I'm really
captivated by the ways

00:23:56.680 --> 00:23:59.860
that we're taught to
interact with algorithms,

00:23:59.860 --> 00:24:03.240
and then, by extension, that
shapes the way we interact

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:04.170
with each other.

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:06.820
So very central to
the work is a critique

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:10.890
of the both technical and
social systems that we're

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:12.720
building around
ourselves, and kind

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:15.420
of poking at and questioning,
what are these rules?

00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:17.930
What happens when we
introduce glitches?

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:19.810
So to give some
examples, I'm often

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:23.000
creating performances that
have some viewer engagement.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:26.270
So it's asking people to
remote-control my dates

00:24:26.270 --> 00:24:29.740
or offering to be their
real-life follower

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:34.330
or providing a service, where
I am acting in their home

00:24:34.330 --> 00:24:36.700
as a human Amazon Alexa.

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:41.760
So I'm making these
performances in which we

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:44.833
both enter into the
system and try to navigate

00:24:44.833 --> 00:24:46.250
and understand our
roles within it

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:47.625
and then hopefully,
by extension,

00:24:47.625 --> 00:24:52.370
maybe understand some of the
larger systems around us.

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:57.840
And I think each one of
these feels like an attempt--

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:00.190
like I'm trying to
almost embody a machine

00:25:00.190 --> 00:25:02.240
or understand the distance
between the algorithm

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:05.610
and myself or the distance
between others and me.

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:09.510
And there are these
breakdowns in that process,

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:11.770
and then just a
lot of questions.

00:25:11.770 --> 00:25:13.640
Who builds these
artificial systems,

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:15.890
and what values do they embody?

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:17.870
Who is prioritized,
and who's targeted

00:25:17.870 --> 00:25:21.830
as we're talking about biases
programmatically encoded?

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:25.700
And then what are the boundaries
around our intimate spaces?

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:28.410
And in the midst of all
these networked interfaces,

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:32.030
what does it mean to be truly
present with one another?

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:34.060
So those are the questions
I'm thinking about.

00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:39.680
But often, there's a high
degree of just awkwardness

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:43.680
and ambiguity in these
moments I'm trying to create.

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:44.430
Yeah.

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:48.060
And, well, I know that one of
the concerns you and I share--

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:50.970
not just you and I, but
I think all four of us--

00:25:50.970 --> 00:25:57.410
is that of enabling people
to be creative, conceptual,

00:25:57.410 --> 00:26:00.760
imaginative, expressive
with computing.

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:03.640
And we talked about
this in the past.

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:07.860
You don't think of maybe
P5.js the last time we talked.

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:12.690
You didn't think of P5.js as
part of your artistic practice.

00:26:12.690 --> 00:26:15.320
But it's a very
significant project

00:26:15.320 --> 00:26:19.960
related to generative
art and enabling, really,

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:23.010
of a community of
people online who

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:25.800
are able to exchange and
deal with their work.

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:30.590
And this really leads
on to the last big topic

00:26:30.590 --> 00:26:32.430
that I wanted us
to talk about some.

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:34.360
We talked about the
artworks a little bit.

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:36.370
We talked about
artists a little bit.

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:39.920
But there's also these
communities and groups

00:26:39.920 --> 00:26:42.480
that this production comes from.

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:46.170
It's not just someone
sitting alone on a computer,

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:50.140
coming up with an idea, and
putting it up on the web.

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:51.810
So can you tell us some?

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:55.710
Doesn't have to be about P5.js
exclusively, but certainly

00:26:55.710 --> 00:26:58.050
that's a really
intriguing project

00:26:58.050 --> 00:26:59.340
I'd love to hear more about.

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:02.510
Can you tell us some
about these communities

00:27:02.510 --> 00:27:08.270
of computational art production
from your perspective, Lauren?

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:09.220
Yeah.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:11.032
I guess I don't think
of it, like you said,

00:27:11.032 --> 00:27:12.490
as [INAUDIBLE]
because I completely

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:15.120
forgot in the last
question to mention it.

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:18.380
But, yeah, it's very related.

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:21.680
And I think what I love about
your question just now is you

00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:23.030
asked about the communities.

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:26.530
And you use the word
"community" rather than "tool."

00:27:26.530 --> 00:27:28.700
So I think a lot of
what we're seeing

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:32.360
is groups of artists coming
together and collaboratively

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:36.390
making tools or
methodologies or practices

00:27:36.390 --> 00:27:38.670
between themselves, where
they're sharing bits of code

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:40.340
or different techniques.

00:27:40.340 --> 00:27:44.410
P5 is one example, but
there's many others.

00:27:44.410 --> 00:27:48.600
But I think your focus on
community is so right on

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:52.520
and what's really apparent
in the works of these artists

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:54.420
in the exhibition, too.

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:57.970
It's one thing to have an
open source piece of code that

00:27:57.970 --> 00:28:00.560
might get shared or used.

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:03.360
And I think it's another to
build a space around that

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:07.820
and to be really intentional
about who is welcomed here.

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:11.430
How are we thinking
about using technology?

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:13.530
How are we thinking
about collaboration?

00:28:13.530 --> 00:28:18.640
How are we building structures
as a community towards a future

00:28:18.640 --> 00:28:21.050
that is more
inclusive or that is

00:28:21.050 --> 00:28:24.010
more supportive of artists
and the sorts of practices

00:28:24.010 --> 00:28:25.240
we want to have?

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:28.340
So yeah, it's really exciting
to see a lot of the artists

00:28:28.340 --> 00:28:30.920
in this exhibition
using open-source tools.

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:33.310
And the fact that these works
will also be open-source

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:34.900
means that others
can build on them.

00:28:34.900 --> 00:28:37.260
And it can be part of
the ongoing conversation

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:40.350
and community.

00:28:40.350 --> 00:28:40.850
Yes.

00:28:40.850 --> 00:28:45.200
Although, as you point out, just
having the code out by itself

00:28:45.200 --> 00:28:48.980
doesn't make a community
happen around it.

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:51.600
It can be an ingredient to that.

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:54.080
So in the open
source world, there's

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:57.550
a festival in Bergen,
Norway, called

00:28:57.550 --> 00:29:02.590
Piksel, which is exclusively
for open-source artwork.

00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:05.550
It's done with computing.

00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:09.270
And so there are
these opportunities,

00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:12.420
these communities,
that maybe aren't

00:29:12.420 --> 00:29:17.820
tied to specific software
tools, platforms, works.

00:29:17.820 --> 00:29:21.660
And then there's also
software tools that--

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:26.880
or platforms where people
sharing their work,

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:31.810
discussing it, coming up with
things is really important.

00:29:31.810 --> 00:29:34.100
So there's a system,
Dwitter, which is really

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:37.050
like a very, very
lightweight addition

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:41.300
to JavaScript-- like a
few abbreviations of math

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:43.550
dot cosine and things like that.

00:29:43.550 --> 00:29:49.110
As a technical feat,
it's not that great.

00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:52.180
But what it's done is
it's enabled people

00:29:52.180 --> 00:29:55.580
to make these concise
Tweet-length programs,

00:29:55.580 --> 00:30:01.180
share them with each other, and
have conversations and build

00:30:01.180 --> 00:30:02.660
community around there.

00:30:02.660 --> 00:30:05.450
People do that with P5.js also.

00:30:05.450 --> 00:30:12.390
But these are the aspects
that I find really intriguing.

00:30:15.230 --> 00:30:20.070
So I'm wondering, Sarah,
from your perspective

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:26.070
if you have some sorts
of communities engaged

00:30:26.070 --> 00:30:28.500
with generative art,
particularly maybe

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:33.300
the new ones that are
represented in the exhibit,

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:37.660
that you want to
highlight for us?

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:41.860
Yeah, and I think for
me what was so important

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:45.570
about this work
is, again, artists

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:50.010
who are playing with
language and decision-making.

00:30:50.010 --> 00:30:55.010
And by including a lot
of people in an event

00:30:55.010 --> 00:30:57.510
that requires
audience participation,

00:30:57.510 --> 00:31:02.250
such as this show, you
really can create a dialogue

00:31:02.250 --> 00:31:04.232
with a lot of different
perspectives, which

00:31:04.232 --> 00:31:06.690
I found very interesting, which
I feel like was highlighted

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:07.790
in a lot of the work.

00:31:07.790 --> 00:31:10.350
And having them all together--
it's almost like they're

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:12.580
having a dialogue
with one another.

00:31:12.580 --> 00:31:14.180
I feel like that's
something that's

00:31:14.180 --> 00:31:17.680
really important and
unique about this work,

00:31:17.680 --> 00:31:21.140
compared to something that
is a unified material object.

00:31:21.140 --> 00:31:24.700
It's something that
exists within a community

00:31:24.700 --> 00:31:28.070
and is also continually
building off of each other.

00:31:28.070 --> 00:31:30.032
But for me, the
dialogue is something

00:31:30.032 --> 00:31:31.490
that's really
important, because we

00:31:31.490 --> 00:31:34.930
need to have these critical
discussions, where we introduce

00:31:34.930 --> 00:31:38.060
different ideas
through emergent form

00:31:38.060 --> 00:31:44.418
to create alternative knowledge
in how we use computation.

00:31:44.418 --> 00:31:45.960
Because it is-- it's
something that's

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:48.540
going to be consistently
evolving over time.

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:51.770
So generating new
narratives, like Fox

00:31:51.770 --> 00:31:53.760
was mentioning all
these things, are

00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:56.920
going to be really critical
in the future of art.

00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:59.010
And I'm curious what
Fox has to say, too.

00:32:01.820 --> 00:32:05.080
Sure, thanks, for
asking me, too.

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:08.210
So I can answer both
in terms of what

00:32:08.210 --> 00:32:11.150
I see for communities, for
the artists in the exhibition,

00:32:11.150 --> 00:32:13.510
and then maybe segue to
some of the communities

00:32:13.510 --> 00:32:16.400
I feel engaged with
in my own work.

00:32:16.400 --> 00:32:21.560
And so Nick earlier was talking
about communities oriented

00:32:21.560 --> 00:32:25.020
around particular types
of software practices,

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:28.480
whether that's open source
software, free software,

00:32:28.480 --> 00:32:30.510
and more.

00:32:30.510 --> 00:32:32.540
At the same time
as when he spoke

00:32:32.540 --> 00:32:34.460
about the different
artists themselves,

00:32:34.460 --> 00:32:36.570
he alluded to other
types of communities,

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:39.260
such as where they're
situated within

00:32:39.260 --> 00:32:41.100
different global communities.

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:43.460
And that might
include communities

00:32:43.460 --> 00:32:48.350
related to issues like gender,
race/ethnicity, nationality,

00:32:48.350 --> 00:32:49.390
and more.

00:32:49.390 --> 00:32:52.850
And so one way that I
like to look at this as--

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:56.040
and I think it's
maybe common now

00:32:56.040 --> 00:32:58.490
to think about intersections
between communities.

00:32:58.490 --> 00:33:00.520
But usually, that's
more for the kind

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:02.147
of social-cultural communities.

00:33:02.147 --> 00:33:03.980
Here, I think we're
looking at intersections

00:33:03.980 --> 00:33:05.860
between those types
of communities

00:33:05.860 --> 00:33:08.570
as well as different types
of software practices.

00:33:08.570 --> 00:33:11.010
So for example, the
difference between

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:14.090
festival-oriented digital
media artists compared

00:33:14.090 --> 00:33:17.420
to artists who are focused
in the gallery, the kind

00:33:17.420 --> 00:33:21.250
of fine arts scene,
that has software arts

00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:23.990
involved, but on the
periphery quite often.

00:33:23.990 --> 00:33:26.760
And electronic literature
as another kind of example.

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:28.700
I know Nick is interested
in the demo scene

00:33:28.700 --> 00:33:30.600
and other types of communities.

00:33:30.600 --> 00:33:32.640
And so I like to
really think about how

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:34.520
all of those
communities intersect

00:33:34.520 --> 00:33:38.010
and then how they
can be generative

00:33:38.010 --> 00:33:40.000
when you think about
these kind of issues.

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:41.720
I'm thinking about work.

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:45.460
I collaborated with
Jason Lewis at Concordia

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:48.140
and other researchers
for a workshop

00:33:48.140 --> 00:33:51.140
on indigenous protocols and
artificial intelligence.

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:54.860
And that's a kind of example of
communities of practice focused

00:33:54.860 --> 00:33:57.100
on AI coming together
with communities that

00:33:57.100 --> 00:33:59.190
are interested in indigeneityi.

00:33:59.190 --> 00:34:02.940
And then just finally, I'll
just say about some of the way

00:34:02.940 --> 00:34:04.490
that I think about communities.

00:34:04.490 --> 00:34:06.840
I feel like there are
a lot of communities

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:09.750
I've been engaged with
in terms of AI practice

00:34:09.750 --> 00:34:14.300
and mathematics, computer
science, digital media arts,

00:34:14.300 --> 00:34:16.310
and fine arts and so forth.

00:34:16.310 --> 00:34:18.679
And one of the reasons
I went into academia

00:34:18.679 --> 00:34:20.960
is that there
weren't always places

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:23.070
where all of those
different constituencies

00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:24.900
all came together naturally.

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:27.050
I think they're
increasingly more.

00:34:27.050 --> 00:34:29.239
But still, they're
underrepresented.

00:34:29.239 --> 00:34:31.739
And so Nick mentioned the
Imagination, Computation,

00:34:31.739 --> 00:34:33.090
and Expression Laboratory.

00:34:33.090 --> 00:34:36.270
I saw that as a place where
some of these kind of issues

00:34:36.270 --> 00:34:39.670
could come together-- issues of
social and cultural analysis,

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:43.130
social change, algorithmic
representation, meaning

00:34:43.130 --> 00:34:45.760
and semantic
representations, and more.

00:34:45.760 --> 00:34:49.389
And now with the MIT Center
for Advanced Virtuality, that I

00:34:49.389 --> 00:34:52.449
was founding director of,
started a couple of years ago,

00:34:52.449 --> 00:34:53.010
here.

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:54.760
I look at that as just
another opportunity

00:34:54.760 --> 00:34:57.640
to continue building these
kind of communities, where

00:34:57.640 --> 00:34:59.850
you bring those
kind of interests

00:34:59.850 --> 00:35:01.903
into intersection with one another.

00:35:01.903 --> 00:35:04.070
So you can have people who
are interested in pushing

00:35:04.070 --> 00:35:06.810
the boundaries of virtual
reality, augmented reality,

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:09.390
other forms of immersive
media, synthetic media,

00:35:09.390 --> 00:35:12.510
but also looking at the
latest, say, race and gender

00:35:12.510 --> 00:35:16.070
critical work and building
systems that embody

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:20.070
and model those kind of
phenomena within the same unit,

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:22.340
or access to education
in STEM, where

00:35:22.340 --> 00:35:23.880
you look at
students' backgrounds

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:27.110
as positive resources for
learning and for social change.

00:35:27.110 --> 00:35:30.170
So I just look at
this increasing path

00:35:30.170 --> 00:35:32.360
towards bringing those
different concerns together

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:34.170
within the same
community, finding others

00:35:34.170 --> 00:35:35.920
who are doing the same,
and then providing

00:35:35.920 --> 00:35:37.960
the opportunities for
people here at MIT

00:35:37.960 --> 00:35:39.668
and more broadly to
do that kind of work.

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:42.780
Yeah.

00:35:42.780 --> 00:35:50.050
I think there's a wide range
of not just communities,

00:35:50.050 --> 00:35:53.420
but also we can talk about
literal spaces in terms

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:58.570
of architectural spaces or the
way that events like festivals

00:35:58.570 --> 00:36:01.150
facilitate types
of interactions.

00:36:01.150 --> 00:36:04.360
I think it's important
not to forget about that,

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:07.900
as we are in our
different circumstances

00:36:07.900 --> 00:36:10.240
today due to the public
health situation,

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:12.430
and to look forward
to how some of those

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:14.020
can be restored and improved.

00:36:14.020 --> 00:36:18.490
Now, Fox came down to
Babycastles Community Gallery

00:36:18.490 --> 00:36:20.980
in New York, which has
representation in the show,

00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:24.860
and kicked us off for
the Synchrony Demo Party

00:36:24.860 --> 00:36:26.060
down there--

00:36:26.060 --> 00:36:28.480
this DIY basement space.

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:35.830
It's very different than the
spaces at MIT or at the Design

00:36:35.830 --> 00:36:38.370
Media Arts Program at
UCLA, which is also

00:36:38.370 --> 00:36:40.730
well-represented in the show.

00:36:40.730 --> 00:36:45.310
So there's this range of
different opportunities

00:36:45.310 --> 00:36:47.790
that people have to
connect with each other,

00:36:47.790 --> 00:36:51.460
to learn from each other's
practices, to develop work.

00:36:51.460 --> 00:36:57.750
And I'm just fascinated
by learning about some

00:36:57.750 --> 00:37:00.630
of these that fit into
the smaller niches--

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:02.900
not just trying to
improve, for instance,

00:37:02.900 --> 00:37:09.250
what we do at MIT and other
top research institutions.

00:37:09.250 --> 00:37:10.880
Just in closing, I
do want to mention

00:37:10.880 --> 00:37:14.460
that this conversation and
having this as a produced

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:20.520
video really was done explicitly
in order to foster community

00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:23.340
and conversation, so
that during our opening,

00:37:23.340 --> 00:37:28.310
instead of interrupting everyone
by having comments from me

00:37:28.310 --> 00:37:32.310
and other people, we'd be
able to put this out, make

00:37:32.310 --> 00:37:35.400
it available to you, hopefully.

00:37:35.400 --> 00:37:39.660
I'd love you to be
able to full-screen it.

00:37:39.660 --> 00:37:44.050
Turn on the closed captions and
watch it at two times speed,

00:37:44.050 --> 00:37:45.870
so you can get
through it and all

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:50.100
download it into your brain,
if you like to do that.

00:37:50.100 --> 00:37:52.440
And then come to
that conversation

00:37:52.440 --> 00:37:57.440
that we're having, come to
the opening, and have exchange

00:37:57.440 --> 00:37:59.880
and discussion
with other people.

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:04.640
Let me see if there's any
final remarks that any of you

00:38:04.640 --> 00:38:08.170
have in the dwindling
seconds that we have, here.

00:38:10.690 --> 00:38:12.780
I just wanted to
congratulate all the artists.

00:38:12.780 --> 00:38:15.580
I know it's a hard
time to be making work.

00:38:15.580 --> 00:38:19.680
And it's a really important time
to be making work and finding

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:20.300
some balance.

00:38:20.300 --> 00:38:22.887
And I think all these artists
are doing that and opening up

00:38:22.887 --> 00:38:24.220
really important questions here.

00:38:27.130 --> 00:38:30.150
[00:36:39.74] That's a very
important thing to state,

00:38:30.150 --> 00:38:30.650
Lauren.

00:38:30.650 --> 00:38:33.220
I want to congratulate
the artists,

00:38:33.220 --> 00:38:37.670
thank them for making the work
that people can experience

00:38:37.670 --> 00:38:39.490
in their browsers,
and also making

00:38:39.490 --> 00:38:42.400
it available as free/libre/
open-source software.

00:38:42.400 --> 00:38:44.680
I know that's a part
of people's practice

00:38:44.680 --> 00:38:47.110
that they called out in
their bios in some cases.

00:38:47.110 --> 00:38:50.410
But whether or not they
started off that way,

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:51.980
everybody agreed to it.

00:38:51.980 --> 00:38:56.227
And as a result, we do
have that contribution.

00:38:59.290 --> 00:39:00.040
I would add just--

00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:00.660
All right--

00:39:00.660 --> 00:39:02.020
--in line with your--

00:39:02.020 --> 00:39:06.000
I would add just in line
with your congratulations

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:09.190
of the artists, Lauren
and Nick, and just

00:39:09.190 --> 00:39:11.750
to say that I also felt very
inspired by the kind of work

00:39:11.750 --> 00:39:13.200
that the artists produced.

00:39:13.200 --> 00:39:15.290
In all of the terms
we mentioned earlier,

00:39:15.290 --> 00:39:17.965
but we also didn't speak
a lot about aesthetics.

00:39:17.965 --> 00:39:19.340
And I just want
to say that there

00:39:19.340 --> 00:39:23.250
is equally broad array of
aesthetic kind of sensibilities

00:39:23.250 --> 00:39:27.340
in the work, from 3D CGI, which
is almost video game-like,

00:39:27.340 --> 00:39:33.260
to more InfoVis work, to more
critical radical socially

00:39:33.260 --> 00:39:35.340
engaged artwork, to glitch art.

00:39:35.340 --> 00:39:38.340
And so I feel like there's
just such a wide array

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:41.550
of aesthetic sensibilities
that are represented,

00:39:41.550 --> 00:39:46.430
along with the
social possibilities,

00:39:46.430 --> 00:39:48.270
along with the
political possibilities,

00:39:48.270 --> 00:39:49.580
along with the communities.

00:39:49.580 --> 00:39:51.550
I just found that
really inspiring

00:39:51.550 --> 00:39:57.720
to work with you, Sarah, Lauren,
and Nick and Parag on this,

00:39:57.720 --> 00:40:00.405
and then, of course, just engage
with the work of these artists.

00:40:05.770 --> 00:40:06.830
Yeah, it's well said.

00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:08.660
And again, thank you
to all the artists.

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:12.770
And it's such an incredible
time to make work, and also

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:13.820
a very challenging time.

00:40:13.820 --> 00:40:16.430
And it's important
that we continue

00:40:16.430 --> 00:40:19.560
to make work that analyzes and
critiques these structures.

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:20.840
And thank you so much.

00:40:23.780 --> 00:40:27.310
All right, and thanks
once again to you

00:40:27.310 --> 00:40:31.900
three and to Parag for
the work on the jury

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:36.810
to figure out which of
the very large number

00:40:36.810 --> 00:40:40.350
of good submissions
we had would be

00:40:40.350 --> 00:40:42.710
able to end up in this
"Generative Unfoldings"

00:40:42.710 --> 00:40:45.600
exhibit and the new work
that we'd be able to get.

00:40:45.600 --> 00:40:47.730
All right, thanks much.

00:40:47.730 --> 00:40:51.460
And we'll look forward
to the opening.

